Answer: theia is an analysis technique that automatically finds structural defects in deep‑learning programs. it builds a library‑agnostic meta‑model of a program—capturing dataset traits (such as dimensions and type of training data) and model attributes (like number of layers, learning rate, activation functions, etc.)—and then applies context‑sensitive analyses (call‑strings and parameter‑sensitive analyses) together with verification rules to locate bugs related to model architecture, loss functions, preprocessing, and similar issues. theia works with popular frameworks such as keras and pytorch and reports the bug location and suggested fixes early in the training process.

neuralint is a prior tool for detecting bugs in deep‑learning code. it also analyzes programs to identify problems, but it focuses on a broader set of issues and, according to evaluations, finds fewer structural bugs than theia. it serves as a baseline for comparing the effectiveness of newer approaches like theia.
